PR #196  overall: potential_risk
Spec: PM4ADDA accumulating multiply-add instructions perform read-modify-write on rd; incorrect aliasing to non-accumulating PM4ADD overwrite variants silently discards prior rd state, causing data corruption.
  [RISK] include/sbi/sbi_trap.h:220-220  SET_RD
      SET_RD unconditionally overwrites rd without reading the prior value. It provides zero safeguard against misuse for accumulating (read-modify-write) instructions. There is no paired convenience macro (e.g. GET_RD) to encourage the correct two-step pattern. A developer adding PM4ADDA emulation who calls SET_RD(insn, regs, product) directly — without first reading rd via REG_VAL and accumulating — would silently discard prior accumulator state. The risk is latent: the macro is currently only used correctly (CSR emulation writes old value, atomics write LR result, loads write fetched data), but nothing in the API prevents the accumulating-vs-overwriting mistake.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_insn.c:48-111  system_opcode_insn
      Correctly implements the read-modify-write pattern for CSR instructions: reads old CSR value at line 71 via sbi_emulate_csr_read(), computes new value incorporating old state (lines 74-101), conditionally writes back (line 103), then SET_RD with the old value (line 106). This is the exact shape a correct PM4ADDA emulation would need (read rd, accumulate product, write back). The pattern is sound; the risk is only that a future PM4 handler added to the same dispatch table would not replicate this discipline.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_illegal_atomic.c:100-121  DEFINE_ATOMIC_FUNCTION
      Establishes the canonical read-modify-write emulation pattern: LR reads prior value into rd_val (line 109), the func argument (e.g. 'rd_val + val') incorporates old state into the computation (line 113), SC writes back the new value atomically, then SET_RD writes the old rd_val to the destination register (line 118). This is the gold-standard pattern that a correct PM4ADDA emulation must follow: read rd, accumulate product into it, write back. A PM4ADDA handler that skipped the read-rd step (the LR equivalent) would realize the vulnerability.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_trap_ldst.c:47-172  sbi_trap_emulate_load
      Demonstrates the 'decode, operate, SET_RD' pattern for load instructions at line 154-160: emu() fetches data into val, then SET_RD writes val directly to rd without ever reading rd's prior value. This is correct for loads (which overwrite rd by ISA definition), but it establishes a dangerous template. A developer modeling a PM4 emulator on this existing pattern would see: (1) decode instruction, (2) perform operation, (3) SET_RD result. For an accumulating multiply-add, step (2) would need to first read rd via REG_VAL, accumulate the product, and only then SET_RD — but the existing load emulation code never shows this read-rd step, so the template is misleading for accumulating instructions.
